{"title":"2012 CEMC Fryer Problems/Problem 2d","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_2\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_CEMC_Fryer_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2012 CEMC Fryer Problems/Problem 2\">Problem 2</a></span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_CEMC_Fryer_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=T-1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The <i>prime factorization</i> of 144 is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/1/d51bb3db158166a782ab9cd802bc38b51f008a92.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\times 2 \\times 2 \\times 2 \\times 3 \\times 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"167\" height=\"15\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/5/be541ec10e87cb878b3666f21e2bf68062c85dd5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{4} \\times 3^{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"57\" height=\"18\" />. Therefore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/f/50ff9540650f0f95f80da2f6692fc8ee413e2eb3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$144$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"27\" height=\"12\" /> is a perfect square because it can be written in the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/5/46597ed49c0211cd119d29e476076fa8cbf62739.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2^{2} \\times 3) \\times (2^{2} \\times 3)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"148\" height=\"21\" />.\n</p><p>The prime factorization of 45 is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/8/8b89cfd0abdb5c9baec504a05f71501c8c45e70d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3^{2} \\times 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"50\" height=\"18\" />. Therefore, 45 is not a perfect square, but <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/3/00386b7bac2de10decaaaeea05ddbe2601b1326f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$45 \\times 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"51\" height=\"15\" /> is a perfect square, because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/f/33fa04c27e489573c63f46637e0619e4418accf4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$45 \\times 5 = 3^{2} \\times 5^{2} = (3 \\times 5) \\times (3 \\times 5)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"286\" height=\"21\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_CEMC_Fryer_Problems/Problem_2d&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A perfect cube is an integer that can be written in the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/9/1f9fbfae51ea9d3a988972f95adab92f4bb23eb4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n^{3}$\" width=\"17\" height=\"15\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is an integer. For example, 8 is a perfect cube since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/e/71e1d81e9d43f49171477ca6e8a8f996ddda7d4f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8 = 2^{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"52\" height=\"17\" />. The product <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/b/28bc2311112e3e96166a9da233e89106ce6a34a6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$112 \\times w$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"65\" height=\"15\" /> is a perfect cube. If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee4b825a2e36ae093ed7be5e4851ef453b34914.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" /> is a positive integer, what is the smallest possible value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee4b825a2e36ae093ed7be5e4851ef453b34914.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" />?\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_CEMC_Fryer_Problems/Problem_2d&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In the prime factorization of a perfect cube, all of the exponents of the prime factors must be divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>From <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_CEMC_Fryer_Problems/Problem_2a\" title=\"2012 CEMC Fryer Problems/Problem 2a\">part a</a>, the prime factorization of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/d/3/dd3907e89ce5b06f13d78589b5d92624a8b41a7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$112$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"12\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/0/81082664dca7784e687568391842024516e4eb98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{4} \\times 7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"50\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>We can see that the next multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> after <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> apart) and the next multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> after <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> apart). Thus, we need <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee4b825a2e36ae093ed7be5e4851ef453b34914.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" /> to be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/c/cccae968244c3aab7d55147a0256e7f2cbfb547a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{2} \\times 7^{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"57\" height=\"18\" /> multiplied by some perfect cube.\n</p><p>This means that the lowest possible value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee4b825a2e36ae093ed7be5e4851ef453b34914.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/7/0671de1d2914ad5174321c3a45a308bdb4f7da1a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{2} \\times 7^{2} = 14^{2} = \\boxed{196}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"170\" height=\"25\" />.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017230514\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.017 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.035 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 106/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 535/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 5049/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    7.958      1 2012_CEMC_Fryer_Problems/Problem_2\n100.00%    7.958      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:36977:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017230514 and revision id 285876. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}